302	//! Insert and overwrite — spec §6 rows `insert_clip` and `overwrite_clip`.
303	//!
304	//! The two ways to bring new material into a cut that is already timed.
305	//! An **insert** makes room for it: the track is cut under the drop point
306	//! and everything from there on moves later by the new clip's length, so
307	//! nothing is lost and every following clip keeps its relative timing. An
308	//! **overwrite** takes room from what is there: the span the new clip needs
309	//! is cleared on that track alone and nothing moves at all, so the cut's
310	//! total length and every other track are untouched — but for a cut clip's
311	//! linked partner, which `split_clips` cuts at the same bound (spec §11
312	//! AU-92).
313	//!
314	//! Both are compositions of ops that already exist — `split_clips`,
315	//! `insert_time`, `add_clip` — and both are ONE op anyway, for two reasons.
316	//! The first is undo: a drop from the media list is one gesture and must be
317	//! one step, and three ops applied in a row are three. The second is that
318	//! the composition has a rule in it that neither half states — an insert
319	//! cuts only the DESTINATION track and then shifts every unlocked one, so
320	//! the straddling clips elsewhere ride the shift whole (spec §11's rule 3)
321	//! while the one under the drop is cut. Written out at each call site, that
322	//! rule would be restated in the timeline, the menu and the MCP handler.
323	//!
324	//! Neither op invents a length: `whole_media_length` is what `add_clip`
325	//! itself uses, so the room an insert opens is exactly the room the clip
326	//! needs.
327	
328	use crate::edit_clip::clip_end;
329	use crate::edit_time::locked_track;
330	use crate::model::Project;
331	use crate::outcome::Outcome;
332	use crate::time::Ticks;
